/** The lung checklist is shared by the parent record and the non-small-cell record: the first 60 days run the same way for both. */
const LUNG_CHECKLIST: ChecklistItem[] = [
  { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Write down the name and number of your lung cancer clinical nurse specialist, and the hospital's 24-hour line, and keep both by the phone.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.1.1) says a lung cancer clinical nurse specialist should be available at all stages of care to support people and, as appropriate, their family members or carers, and (1.19.3) that people should know how to contact them between hospital visits.", source: NG122_CNS, also: NHS_LUNG_TESTS },
  { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask which type of lung cancer it is (non-small-cell or small-cell), what stage, and whether the aim of treatment is cure or control.", why: "The NHS says treatment depends on the size of the cancer and where it is, the type, whether it has spread, whether the cancer has certain genetic changes and your general health, and that where the cancer cannot be cured the aim is to slow it down, help with symptoms and help you live longer.", source: NHS_LUNG_TREATMENT },
  { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask whether enough tissue was taken for the molecular tests, and which gene panel was sent.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.2.11) says samples should be adequate, without unacceptable risk to the person, to permit pathological diagnosis including tumour subtyping and assessment of molecular markers, and (1.2.12) points to the National Genomics Test Directory for the sequencing panels that guide treatment.", source: NG122_DIAGNOSIS },
  { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "If you smoke or recently stopped, ask to be referred to a stop smoking service, and ask whether it changes the timing of any operation.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.3.1 and 1.3.2) says to inform people that smoking increases the risk of lung complications after surgery and to advise stopping as soon as the diagnosis is suspected, and (1.3.3) not to postpone surgery to allow people to stop, so the offer is a treatment with a reason rather than a condition; NHS Better Health lists local stop smoking services, nicotine replacement and stop smoking medicines, offers a free Personal Quit Plan and says reaching 28 days smoke free makes you 5 times more likely to quit for good.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT, also: NHS_QUIT_SMOKING },
  { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Staging tests: a contrast CT of the chest, liver, adrenals and lower neck, usually a bronchoscopy or needle biopsy, and a PET-CT if treatment aimed at cure is possible.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.2.2) says to offer a contrast-enhanced chest CT including the liver, adrenals and lower neck, (1.2.4) that all people who could potentially have treatment with curative intent are offered PET-CT before treatment, and (1.2.8) to offer endobronchial ultrasound-guided needle aspiration for the nodes near the airways.", source: NG122_DIAGNOSIS, also: NHS_LUNG_TESTS },
  { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "If an operation or radical radiotherapy is being considered, ask for your breathing test results and what they mean.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.4.9) says to perform spirometry and transfer factor testing before treatment with curative intent, (1.4.11) to perform a functional segment count to predict lung function after surgery, and (1.4.1) to use a global risk score such as Thoracoscore and make sure you are aware of the risk before you consent.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
  { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Learn the three things that help breathlessness today: a handheld fan pointed at your face, relaxed tummy breathing, and a supported forward lean.", why: "Cancer Research UK describes relaxed tummy breathing practised for 10 minutes twice a day when you are not breathless, recovery breathing with a fan and a forward lean for when you are, and pursed lips breathing; NICE NG122 (1.15.3 and 1.15.4) says to consider breathing control and coping strategies delivered by someone with expertise in the techniques, available in all care settings.", source: CRUK_LUNG_BREATHLESSNESS, also: NG122_PALLIATIVE },
  { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Tell the team about any breathlessness, cough, pain, weight loss or appetite change now, rather than saving it for the next clinic.", why: "Macmillan says to contact your doctor straight away if breathlessness is a new symptom, if you have pain when you breathe, or if it gets worse quickly; NICE NG122 (1.18.1) says weight loss, loss of appetite, depression and difficulty swallowing should be managed by multidisciplinary groups including supportive and palliative care professionals.", source: MAC_LUNG_BREATHLESSNESS, also: NG122_PALLIATIVE },
  { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask when your case goes to the lung multidisciplinary team, and whether you will be seen by both a thoracic surgeon and an oncologist.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.6.1) says all people whose condition is potentially suitable for multimodality treatment should be assessed by a thoracic oncologist and by a thoracic surgeon, so seeing both is the standard rather than a second opinion.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
  { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask when the molecular and PD-L1 results are due, and whether treatment should wait for them.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.2.12) points teams at the National Genomics Test Directory panels, and its advanced disease treatment pathways are organised by PD-L1 below or at or above 50 percent, so these two results decide between tablets, immunotherapy and chemotherapy.", source: NG122_DIAGNOSIS, also: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
  { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you, here or elsewhere, and what taking part would involve.", why: "The NHS says you can ask your doctor or a patient organisation about clinical trials you may be eligible to join, that you would usually be randomly assigned to the new treatment or to a control group, and that you can leave at any point without giving a reason and without it affecting your care.", source: NHS_TRIALS },
  { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Sort the practical things while you feel able: work, sick pay, benefits, travel costs and a Blue Badge.", why: "Cancer Research UK lists money matters, benefits, sick pay, prescription costs, grants, work issues, childcare, Blue Badge applications, help with travel costs and home adaptations, says a hospital social worker can help, and says getting help early stops these becoming a bigger issue later; Roy Castle has sections on employment rights and financial support.", source: CRUK_LUNG_COPING, also: ROY_CASTLE_LIVING },
  { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Put the red cards on the fridge: struggling to breathe, coughing up more than a few streaks of blood, a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C, swelling of the face or neck, new leg weakness with back pain.", why: "The NHS says to call 999 if you are struggling to breathe or coughing up more than a few spots or streaks of blood; Macmillan says to ring the 24-hour number for a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C or feeling unwell with a normal temperature, and for breathlessness, a new cough or wheezing on immunotherapy.", source: NHS_LUNG_SYMPTOMS, also: MAC_LUNG_PEMBRO },
  { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "If you are on immunotherapy, carry the patient alert card and know that reactions can start after treatment ends.", why: "Macmillan says your team gives you a patient alert card and 24-hour contact numbers, that you can call at any time of day or night, and that immune side effects can start during treatment or after treatment ends.", source: MAC_LUNG_PEMBRO },
  { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask to be referred to palliative care alongside your cancer treatment if the cancer has spread.", why: "A randomised trial in newly diagnosed metastatic non-small-cell lung cancer found that early palliative care alongside standard oncology care improved quality of life, halved depressive symptoms (16 against 38 percent) and lengthened median survival (11.6 against 8.9 months); Marie Curie says palliative care can be given alongside treatments aimed at controlling the illness.", source: TEMEL_2010_LUNG, also: MARIE_CURIE_PALLIATIVE },
  { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your follow-up: an appointment with a specialist within 6 weeks of finishing treatment, then regular appointments arranged for you.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.19.1) says to offer everyone with lung cancer an initial follow-up appointment with a specialist within 6 weeks of completing treatment and regular appointments after that, rather than relying on the person requesting appointments when they have symptoms, and (1.19.2) that nurse-led protocol-driven follow-up is an option.", source: NG122_FOLLOW_UP },
  { when: "By day 60", item: "Find your people: Roy Castle's nurse-staffed line and groups, Cancer Research UK's nurse line and forum, and your nearest Maggie's centre.", why: "Roy Castle Lung Cancer Foundation offers support online, in person and by phone, including a telephone line staffed by lung cancer nurses, information days and support groups; Cancer Research UK says specialist nurses are usually the first point of call if you are finding it difficult to cope.", source: ROY_CASTLE_SUPPORT, also: CRUK_LUNG_COPING },
  { when: "By day 60", item: "If the stigma around lung cancer is making it harder to ask for help, say so to your nurse specialist. Nothing about your care depends on whether you smoked.", why: "Cancer Research UK lists guilt among the common feelings after a lung cancer diagnosis and says specialist nurses can get you the help you need; NICE NG122 (1.3.3) says not to postpone surgery to allow people to stop smoking, which is the guideline saying plainly that treatment is not conditional.", source: CRUK_LUNG_COPING, also: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
];

export const FIRST_60_DAYS_CHECKLISTS: Record<string, ChecklistItem[]> = {
  prostate: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Write down the name and number of your prostate cancer clinical nurse specialist and the hospital's 24-hour line, and add Prostate Cancer UK's specialist nurses on 0800 074 8383.", why: "Every urgent card on this page starts with one of those numbers, and the charity's line is free and staffed by nurses for the questions that arrive at nine in the evening rather than in clinic.", source: PCUK_NURSES, also: NHS_PC_SUPPORT_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask for your Gleason score or grade group, your PSA, your T stage and your Cambridge Prognostic Group, in writing.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.2.15) asks the urological cancer multidisciplinary team to assign a risk category to everyone newly diagnosed with localised or locally advanced disease, and writes a separate treatment recommendation for each of the five groups, so this number decides what is on offer.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask whether the aim is cure or control, and whether the word being used is active surveillance or watchful waiting.", why: "NICE NG131 defines active surveillance as part of a curative strategy that keeps you inside the window of curability, and watchful waiting as a strategy for controlling rather than curing, avoiding surgery and radiation and deferring hormone therapy. The two words sound alike and mean opposite things.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask how long you have to decide. For most localised prostate cancer, a few weeks changes nothing.", why: "Prostate Cancer UK's page on choosing a treatment is built around taking the time to weigh the options with the people around you; NICE NG131 (1.1.5) asks the team to find out how involved you want to be in the decision and to make sure you have enough information to be.", source: PCUK_CHOOSING, also: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask for box 2 of NICE NG131, the table of benefits and harms, and go through it with someone.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.7) says to use box 2 to discuss the benefits and harms of active surveillance, prostatectomy and radical radiotherapy with people with CPG 1, 2 and 3 disease, and (1.1.6) to use an up-to-date decision aid in all urological cancer multidisciplinary teams. It is the conversation with numbers in it rather than adjectives.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask to be seen by both a surgeon and a clinical oncologist before you choose.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.5) says people who are candidates for radical treatment should have the opportunity to discuss the range of treatment modalities and their serious side effects with a specialist surgical oncologist and a specialist clinical oncologist. Seeing both is the standard, not a second opinion you have to argue for.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask what each option would do to your continence and your erections, and ask for the numbers rather than the word side effects.", why: "In the ProtecT trial, pad use rose from 1 percent before treatment to 46 percent six months after surgery and was still 17 percent at six years, against 4 to 5 percent in the other two groups; 67 percent of men could get an erection firm enough for intercourse beforehand, and at six months that was 12 percent after surgery, 22 percent after radiotherapy and 52 percent on monitoring.", source: PROTECT_PRO_F60, also: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask whether sperm storage has been offered, whatever your age.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.2) says to explain the potential loss of ejaculation and fertility associated with radical treatment and to offer sperm storage, and (1.4.7) repeats the offer for men starting long-term hormone therapy.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "If you are having surgery, start pelvic floor muscle exercises now, four to six weeks before the operation.", why: "Prostate Cancer UK says it is a good idea to start the exercises four to six weeks before surgery so you can do them properly afterwards, that it might help you stop leaking sooner, that they are not done while the catheter is in, and that you start again as soon as it comes out.", source: PCUK_PELVIC_FLOOR_F60 },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask to be referred to the erectile dysfunction service before treatment rather than afterwards.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.36 to 1.3.38) sets out tablets first, then vacuum devices, intraurethral inserts, injections or a prosthesis, with access to specialist erectile dysfunction services; Prostate Cancer UK says treatments work better started soon after treatment and that keeping blood flowing to the penis helps preserve it. All of it is free on the NHS with no age limit.", source: NG131_PROSTATE, also: PCUK_SEX_F60 },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "If hormone therapy is part of the plan, ask how many months, and what is being done about your bones, your heart and your weight.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.22) offers six months of androgen deprivation with radical radiotherapy for CPG 2 to 5 and (1.3.23) considers up to three years for CPG 4 and 5; (1.4.13) says to consider assessing fracture risk, (1.4.14) to offer bisphosphonates where osteoporosis is present, and (1.4.19) to offer supervised resistance and aerobic exercise at least twice a week for 12 weeks.", source: NG131_PROSTATE, also: PCUK_HORMONE_F60 },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you, here or elsewhere, and what taking part would involve.", why: "NICE NG131 names trials at several points rather than as a last resort, including (1.3.28) for high-intensity focused ultrasound and cryotherapy, (1.3.32) for immediate post-operative radiotherapy and (1.3.58) for biochemical relapse; the NHS says you can leave a trial at any point without giving a reason and without it affecting your care.", source: NG131_PROSTATE, also: NHS_PC_TREATMENT_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Put the red cards on the fridge, with spinal cord compression at the top.", why: "Prostate Cancer UK says about 4 in 100 people with prostate cancer develop metastatic spinal cord compression, to get medical advice straight away for new back pain, leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder or bowel control, not to wait to see if it settles and not to worry that it is the evening or the weekend; NICE NG234 (1.3.2) treats it as an oncological emergency and (1.5.2) asks for an MRI within 24 hours.", source: PCUK_MSCC_F60, also: NG234_MSCC_PROSTATE },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask the team to write down who to contact during the day, at night and at the weekend.", why: "Prostate Cancer UK asks specifically for this, because MSCC can be mistaken for ordinary back pain and because if you cannot reach anyone the instruction is to go to A and E and say you have prostate cancer and symptoms of spinal cord compression.", source: PCUK_MSCC_F60 },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Start or keep up regular exercise, including something that loads the muscles.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.4.19) says to offer people starting or having androgen deprivation therapy supervised resistance and aerobic exercise at least twice a week for 12 weeks to reduce fatigue and improve quality of life; Prostate Cancer UK says gentle resistance exercise helps prevent muscle loss and may help prevent the falls that cause fractures.", source: NG131_PROSTATE, also: PCUK_ACTIVITY_F60 },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your PSA follow-up: no earlier than six weeks after radical treatment, then at least every six months for two years, then yearly.", why: "NICE NG131 (1.3.47) sets that timetable, (1.3.48) says not to do a routine rectal examination while the PSA stays at baseline, and (1.3.49) allows remote follow-up after the first six months for people with a stable PSA and no significant complications.", source: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Say out loud, to one person, how you are actually doing.", why: "Prostate Cancer UK says it is normal to feel scared, worried, stressed, helpless or angry, that there is no right way to feel, and that hormone therapy itself can cause low mood and anxiety; NICE NG131 (1.1.13) offers you and your partner the chance to talk to a healthcare professional experienced in psychosexual issues at any stage.", source: PCUK_EMOTIONAL_F60, also: NG131_PROSTATE },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Find your people: Prostate Cancer UK's one-to-one peer support and online community, Macmillan, and your nearest Maggie's centre.", why: "The NHS lists Prostate Cancer UK, Cancer Research UK, Macmillan and the Prostate Cancer Research infopool as the national sources of support, and Prostate Cancer UK runs a specialist nurse line, one-to-one peer support, a Black men's support service and an online community.", source: NHS_PC_SUPPORT_PROSTATE, also: PCUK_JUST_DIAGNOSED },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Remember that whatever you choose, the trial that settled this found the same survival at fifteen years.", why: "In ProtecT, death from prostate cancer occurred in 17 of 545 men (3.1 percent) on active monitoring, 12 of 553 (2.2 percent) after prostatectomy and 16 of 545 (2.9 percent) after radiotherapy, with the overall comparison not significant, and 133 men in the monitoring group were alive at the end of follow-up with no prostate cancer treatment at all. Those are figures for a trial population, not a forecast for one man.", source: PROTECT_15Y_F60 },
  ],
  gallbladder: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Get the name and number of your clinical nurse specialist (your key worker) and the hospital's 24-hour line, and keep both by the phone.", why: "The NHS says a group of specialists looks after you throughout, with a clinical nurse specialist as your main contact; the 24-hour line is where fever, jaundice or uncontrolled pain are reported.", source: NHS_GB_TESTS },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "If the cancer was found after your gallbladder was removed, ask for a copy of the pathology report: the T stage and the cystic duct margin decide whether a second operation is advised.", why: "Cancer Research UK says a second operation may be suggested; the systematic review of incidental gallbladder cancer supports re-resection for T1b and deeper tumours in fit patients.", source: CRUK_DECISIONS, also: SOREIDE },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask when your case goes to the hepatobiliary multidisciplinary team meeting and how you will hear what was decided.", why: "Macmillan explains that a team of specialists meets to agree the recommended plan before it is put to you.", source: MAC_MDT },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Staging tests: blood tests, a CT scan of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, often MRI, sometimes PET, laparoscopy or ERCP.", why: "The NHS lists these tests and says it can take several weeks to get the results, which does not mean something is wrong.", source: NHS_GB_TESTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "If your skin or eyes are yellow, ask how and when the blockage will be relieved (usually a stent) and what the signs of a blocked or infected stent are.", why: "Cancer Research UK says stents can block after a few months and that a high temperature or shivering means contacting your doctor straight away.", source: CRUK_STENTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask for a dietitian referral if you have lost weight or your appetite; nutritional drinks are available on prescription.", why: "Cancer Research UK says many people with gallbladder cancer lose their appetite and some lose weight, and that a dietitian can advise.", source: CRUK_EATING },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Decide what to tell your employer, check your sick pay, and ask Macmillan's welfare rights advisers what you can claim.", why: "Macmillan says the law treats cancer as a disability, employers should make reasonable adjustments, and Statutory Sick Pay applies if there is no company scheme.", source: MAC_WORK, also: MAC_BENEFITS },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Surgeon appointment: is the cancer resectable, what operation, which unit and how many it does a year; ask about a second opinion or referral to a hepatobiliary centre.", why: "AMMF advises care from a team specialising in the hepatobiliary system and lists UK centres; Macmillan explains how a second opinion is arranged.", source: AMMF_CENTRES, also: MAC_SECOND },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "If the cancer has spread, ask that HER2 testing and a tumour gene panel are requested now, on tissue already taken, so the results are back before a treatment change is needed.", why: "The NHS says your hospital specialist requests genomic testing on a sample already removed; AMMF's booklet explains why a result may open a targeted treatment.", source: NHS_GENOMIC, also: AMMF_PROFILING },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you and what joining would involve.", why: "The NHS clinical trials page lists the questions to ask before joining and says you can leave at any time without it affecting your care.", source: NHS_TRIALS },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Second operation, if advised: ask about timing and about prehabilitation (exercise, nutrition, stopping smoking) while you wait.", why: "A US multicentre study found survival was best when re-resection was done around four to eight weeks after the first operation; Cancer Research UK describes prehabilitation before treatment.", source: ETHUN, also: CRUK_PREHAB },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Oncologist appointment for cancer that has spread: gemcitabine and cisplatin with durvalumab is the NHS standard; ask about the aim, the side effects, the alert card and the 24-hour number.", why: "NICE TA944 recommends durvalumab with gemcitabine and cisplatin for unresectable or advanced biliary tract cancer.", source: NICE_TA944 },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask for the palliative care or symptom control team to be involved alongside treatment if you have pain, itching, sickness or weight loss.", why: "The NHS says people whose cancer cannot be cured are referred to the palliative care or symptom control team; it can work alongside active treatment.", source: NHS_GB_TREATMENT },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Chemotherapy after surgery: ask whether six months of capecitabine tablets is recommended and when it should start.", why: "The BILCAP trial made six months of capecitabine the standard after biliary cancer surgery.", source: BILCAP },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Carers: tell the team you are the carer, ask the council for a free carer's assessment, and check whether Carer's Allowance applies.", why: "The NHS says anyone over 18 can ask for a carer's assessment; Carer's Allowance is £86.45 a week for 35 or more hours of care for someone on certain benefits.", source: NHS_CARER_ASSESSMENT, also: NHS_CARER_BENEFITS },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your follow-up plan and contact the team about any new symptom between visits.", why: "Cancer Research UK says follow-up after curative treatment is typically every 3 months for 2 years then every 6 months, with blood tests at most visits and scans when needed.", source: CRUK_FOLLOWUP },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Have the printed red cards, the medicine list and the date any stent went in to hand, and know the free helplines.", why: "The NHS lists Macmillan (0808 808 00 00), Cancer Research UK nurses (0808 800 4040), Maggie's and Marie Curie; Macmillan says carers should tell the team who they are.", source: NHS_GB_SUPPORT, also: MAC_CARERS },
  ],
  tnbc: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Get the name and number of your breast care nurse (your key worker) and the hospital's 24-hour line, and keep both by the phone and in your mobile.", why: "NICE NG101 says everyone with breast cancer should have a named clinical nurse specialist; the NHS says this nurse is your main point of contact during and after treatment, and Macmillan says to keep the 24-hour helpline number with you at all times.", source: NHS_BC_TESTS, also: MAC_SEPSIS },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask for a copy of the pathology report and check the three results that define triple-negative: ER, PR and the exact HER2 score (0, 1+ or 2+).", why: "Breast Cancer Now explains that triple negative means all three are negative; a HER2 score of 1+ or 2+ (HER2-low) can open trastuzumab deruxtecan later, so the exact number matters.", source: CRUK_TNBC },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "If having children in future matters to you, say so now and ask for a fertility clinic referral this week, before chemotherapy is booked.", why: "Cancer Research UK says egg or embryo freezing takes about 2 to 3 weeks and that national guidelines expect the team to discuss fertility at diagnosis; NICE NG101 asks teams to advise on fertility preservation.", source: CRUK_FERTILITY, also: NG101 },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask when your case goes to the breast multidisciplinary team meeting and how you will hear what was decided.", why: "NICE NG101 says systemic treatment recommendations are made at the multidisciplinary team meeting and decisions are then made with you after discussing the benefits and risks.", source: NG101 },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Staging: ask whether you need a CT, MRI or PET scan and blood tests before treatment, and when the results will be back.", why: "The NHS lists scans and blood tests as possible next steps after diagnosis; results show how far the cancer has spread and help choose the first treatment.", source: NHS_BC_TESTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask for genetic testing for BRCA1, BRCA2 and PALB2 even with no family history, and how long the result will take.", why: "Breast Cancer Now says anyone diagnosed with triple negative breast cancer under 60 should be offered a referral to discuss testing regardless of family history; NICE NG101 asks for BRCA testing under 50. Results usually take 1 to 3 months and the result can change surgery and open olaparib.", source: BCN_GENETIC, also: NG101 },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask whether chemotherapy will come before surgery, whether pembrolizumab will be added, and what rules you in or out.", why: "Macmillan says people with triple negative breast cancer are more likely to have chemotherapy before surgery; NICE TA851 funds pembrolizumab with chemotherapy before surgery and alone afterwards for early disease at high risk of recurrence or locally advanced disease.", source: MAC_TNBC, also: TA851 },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Decide what to tell your employer, check your sick pay, and ask Macmillan's money advisers what you can claim.", why: "Treatment runs for most of a year. Macmillan says the law treats cancer as a disability, that employers must consider reasonable adjustments once they know, and that its money advice is free.", source: MAC_TNBC, also: NHS_BC_SUPPORT },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Oncologist appointment: ask for the regimen written down (drugs, cycle length, how many weeks before surgery), which side effects to expect and the temperature rule for ringing.", why: "Macmillan describes the KEYNOTE-522 regimen as two 12-week phases before surgery then pembrolizumab alone afterwards, and says to ring the 24-hour line for a temperature over 37.5 C or below 36 C, or for feeling unwell even with a normal temperature.", source: MAC_KN522, also: MAC_SEPSIS },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether your unit offers scalp cooling with your regimen, and if you want a wig, get it fitted before the first cycle.", why: "In the SCALP randomised trial half of women cooled kept more than half their hair against none of the controls; Macmillan says a wig is best matched to your own hair before treatment starts.", source: NANGIA, also: MAC_HAIR },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Buy a thermometer, learn the sepsis signs, and put the printed red cards where the household can see them.", why: "Macmillan says to buy a thermometer and that a minor infection can become life-threatening within hours when white cells are low; the NHS says call 999 for fast breathing, confusion, mottled skin or a very high or low temperature.", source: MAC_SEPSIS },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "If pembrolizumab is part of your treatment, make sure you are given the alert card and know the immune-related symptoms on it.", why: "Breast Cancer Now says you should carry an alert card at all times while having pembrolizumab, listing the symptoms to report and who to contact out of hours.", source: BCN_PEMBRO },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you now, before the first treatment, and what joining would involve.", why: "The Triple Negative Breast Cancer Foundation advises thinking about a trial before first-line treatment; NICE NG101 asks teams to discuss research and encourage trial entry.", source: TNBCF_NEWLY, also: NG101 },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Surgeon appointment (it may come after chemotherapy): breast-conserving surgery or mastectomy, reconstruction now or later, and which lymph node operation.", why: "NICE NG101 says offer both immediate and delayed reconstruction, including to women who may need radiotherapy; the NHS says recovery from a mastectomy takes several weeks with wounds healing in about 6 weeks.", source: NG101, also: NHS_MASTECTOMY },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask how the pathology after chemotherapy will be reported (complete response or residual cancer burden) and what each result would lead to.", why: "In the Symmans series 10-year relapse-free survival for triple-negative disease was 86% after a complete response and 81%, 55% and 23% across RCB classes I to III; residual disease opens capecitabine, or olaparib for BRCA carriers (NICE TA886).", source: SYMMANS, also: TA886 },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Ask about radiotherapy: how many sessions (5 over 1 week or 15 over 3 weeks), to which areas, and about lymphoedema risk and the arm exercises after node surgery.", why: "NICE NG101 sets out 26 Gy in 5 fractions or 40 Gy in 15; it also says people should be told their lymphoedema risk before treatment, and Macmillan says the early signs are a tight watch, ring or sleeve.", source: CRUK_RT, also: MAC_LYMPH },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Carers: tell the team you are the carer, ask the council for a free carer's assessment, and check whether Carer's Allowance applies.", why: "The NHS says anyone over 18 can ask for a carer's assessment; Carer's Allowance is £86.45 a week for 35 or more hours of care for someone on certain benefits.", source: NHS_CARER_ASSESSMENT, also: NHS_CARER_BENEFITS },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know who to talk to when it gets hard: your breast care nurse, Breast Cancer Now's Someone Like Me, Macmillan's line, Maggie's centres, and the TNBC Foundation forum.", why: "The NHS lists Breast Cancer Now (0808 800 6000), Macmillan (0808 808 00 00) and Cancer Research UK nurses (0808 800 4040); Breast Cancer Now says talking to someone outside family and friends can be easier.", source: NHS_BC_SUPPORT, also: BCN_COPING },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Have the printed red cards, the medicine list, the alert card and the 24-hour number to hand, and know that the treatment plan should be written down for you.", why: "NICE NG101 says everyone treated for breast cancer should have an agreed written care plan with named professionals, signs to look out for and contact details for immediate referral; the NHS says talk to your specialists about any symptom without waiting for the next check-up.", source: NG101, also: NHS_BC_TREATMENT },
  ],
  pancreatic: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Get the name and number of your clinical nurse specialist (your key worker) and the hospital's 24-hour line, and keep both by the phone and in your mobile.", why: "The NHS says your team will include a clinical nurse specialist who is your main point of contact during and after treatment; Pancreatic Cancer UK says if you have not been given a key contact, ask the doctor who to contact with questions.", source: NHS_PC_TESTS, also: PCUK_DIAGNOSED },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask which word the team gave your scan, resectable, borderline resectable, locally advanced or metastatic, and what on the scan decided it.", why: "Cancer Research UK says the team first looks at your scans to see if they can remove the cancer, and that word decides whether treatment starts with an operation, with chemotherapy first, or with treatment to control the cancer.", source: CRUK_PC_DECISIONS },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "If your eyes or skin are yellow, ask how and when the blockage will be relieved (straight to surgery, or a stent) and learn the signs of a blocked or infected stent.", why: "NICE NG85 says offer surgery rather than drainage first when the cancer is resectable and you are well enough, and a metal stent otherwise; Pancreatic Cancer UK says jaundice takes two to three weeks to clear and that stents can block or become infected.", source: PCUK_JAUNDICE, also: PCUK_STENT_BILE },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask for pancreatic enzyme capsules (PERT) now if you have weight loss, bloating, wind or pale, oily, floating stools, and ask for a dietitian referral.", why: "Pancreatic Cancer UK says most people with pancreatic cancer will need PERT and to ask if you have digestion symptoms and have not been prescribed it; NICE NG85 says offer pancreatin in unresectable disease and consider it before and after surgery.", source: PCUK_PERT_HOW, also: NG85 },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask when your case goes to the specialist pancreatic cancer multidisciplinary team meeting and how you will hear what was decided.", why: "NICE NG85 says a specialist pancreatic cancer multidisciplinary team should make a shared decision with you about the care that is needed; Macmillan explains the team agrees a recommended plan before it is put to you.", source: NG85, also: MAC_MDT },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Staging: a pancreas-protocol CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, often a PET-CT or endoscopic ultrasound with a biopsy, sometimes a laparoscopy; ask when the results will be back.", why: "NICE NG85 lists these tests; the NHS says results can take several weeks and that this does not mean something is wrong; Pancreatic Cancer UK says a biopsy is needed before chemotherapy or a trial.", source: NHS_PC_TESTS, also: NG85 },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask for germline (inherited) genetic testing and whether tumour testing (KRAS, fusions, mismatch repair) has been requested on tissue already taken.", why: "The OnCo record asks for a germline panel for everyone diagnosed; NICE NG85 asks about relatives with pancreatic cancer; a BRCA result can change chemotherapy, open olaparib and matter for relatives; the NHS says the specialist requests genomic testing on a sample already removed.", source: NHS_GENOMIC, also: PCUK_FAMILY_HISTORY },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Learn the sepsis signs and the temperature rule, buy a thermometer, and put the printed red cards where the household can see them.", why: "A blocked bile duct and chemotherapy are two routes to sepsis in this cancer; Macmillan says to ring the 24-hour line for a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C, and the NHS says call 999 for fast breathing, confusion, blotchy skin or a rash that does not fade.", source: MAC_FOLFIRINOX, also: PCUK_CLOTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Decide what to tell your employer, check your sick pay, and ask Macmillan's money advisers what you can claim; if the cancer cannot be cured ask about the SR1 form.", why: "Pancreatic Cancer UK says your employer must make reasonable adjustments, that PIP, Attendance Allowance and Carer's Allowance may apply, that an SR1 form speeds claims, and that prescriptions are free in England with an FP92A certificate for anyone having cancer treatment.", source: PCUK_WORK_MONEY, also: MAC_WORK },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Surgeon appointment (resectable or borderline): which operation, how many the unit does a year, whether chemotherapy comes first, what recovery looks like, and whether there is a prehabilitation programme.", why: "Pancreatic Cancer UK says surgery is the best treatment for cancer that has not spread and that some hospitals offer prehabilitation; Macmillan says only a specialist surgeon experienced in pancreatic surgery does this operation and you may be referred to a specialist centre.", source: PCUK_WHO_SURGERY, also: MAC_SECOND },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Oncologist appointment (chemotherapy first or cancer that cannot be removed): which regimen and why for your fitness, the DPD blood test, the exact rules for ringing, and whether a blood thinner is advised.", why: "NICE NG85 chooses FOLFIRINOX, a gemcitabine combination or gemcitabine alone by fitness; Macmillan says a DPD test comes before fluorouracil; Pancreatic Cancer UK says your oncologist should consider blood-thinning medicine during chemotherapy because clot risk is high in this cancer.", source: MAC_PC_CHEMO, also: PCUK_CHEMO_SIDE },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you now, before the first treatment, and what joining would involve.", why: "NICE NG85 places chemotherapy before surgery for resectable and borderline disease within trials; Cancer Research UK says your doctor may offer a trial for borderline disease; the NHS clinical trials page lists the questions to ask and says you can leave at any time.", source: NHS_TRIALS, also: CRUK_PC_DECISIONS },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "If you have pain, ask what is causing it, which painkillers and laxatives to take, and whether a coeliac plexus block or the palliative care team should be involved now.", why: "Pancreatic Cancer UK says asking for help early makes pain easier to deal with; NICE NG85 says consider a coeliac plexus block for uncontrolled pain, unacceptable opioid side effects or escalating doses.", source: PCUK_PAIN, also: NG85 },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Dietitian appointment: your enzyme dose for meals, snacks and drinks, your weight target, supplement drinks, and a check for diabetes (thirst, passing urine more, tiredness).", why: "Pancreatic Cancer UK gives starting doses of about 50,000 to 75,000 units with a main meal and 25,000 to 50,000 with a snack, reviewed and increased; it says pancreatic cancer or surgery can cause type 3c diabetes, managed by a diabetes nurse and specialist dietitian.", source: PCUK_PERT_HOW, also: PCUK_DIABETES },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask for the palliative care or symptom control team to be involved alongside treatment if you have pain, sickness, itch, poor appetite or low mood.", why: "The NHS says people whose cancer cannot be cured are referred to the palliative care or symptom control team, which works alongside treatment; Pancreatic Cancer UK says these services are available at any point, not just at the end of life.", source: NHS_PC_TREATMENT, also: PCUK_INOPERABLE },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "After surgery: ask which chemotherapy will follow, when it needs to start (within 12 weeks), and how eating, enzymes and blood sugar will be sorted so they do not delay it.", why: "PRODIGE 24 made six months of modified FOLFIRINOX the standard for fit patients after surgery; Cancer Research UK and Pancreatic Cancer UK say adjuvant chemotherapy should start within 3 months of surgery and lasts up to 6 months.", source: PRODIGE24, also: PCUK_CHEMO_USE },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Carers: tell the team you are the carer, ask the council for a free carer's assessment, and check whether Carer's Allowance applies.", why: "The NHS says anyone over 18 can ask for a carer's assessment; Pancreatic Cancer UK has a booklet for carers and its nurses take calls from family members; Macmillan says carers should tell the team who they are.", source: NHS_CARER_ASSESSMENT, also: PCUK_FAMILIES },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your follow-up plan (scans about every three months on chemotherapy; CT every 6 months for 5 years after surgery) and contact the team about any new symptom between visits.", why: "Cancer Research UK says after surgery you usually have CT scans every 6 months for 5 years with blood tests and sometimes tumour markers; Pancreatic Cancer UK says a scan every three months on chemotherapy.", source: CRUK_PC_FOLLOW_UP, also: PCUK_CHEMO_USE },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Have the printed red cards, the medicine list with your enzyme doses, the date any stent went in and the 24-hour number to hand, and know who to talk to when it gets hard.", why: "The NHS lists Pancreatic Cancer UK (0808 801 0707, with a nurse service, forum and local groups), Macmillan (0808 808 00 00), Cancer Research UK nurses (0808 800 4040), Maggie's and Marie Curie; Cancer Research UK says a dietitian follows you closely and to check with them about supplements and diabetes.", source: NHS_PC_SUPPORT, also: CRUK_PC_DIET },
  ],
  colorectal: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Get the name and number of your colorectal clinical nurse specialist and the hospital's 24-hour line, and keep both by the phone.", why: "The NHS says your specialist treatment team explains the treatments and helps you manage side effects; every urgent card starts with ringing that number rather than waiting.", source: NHS_BOWEL_TREATMENT },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Write down whether the cancer is in the colon, the rectum or both, and the stage and grade, and ask what they mean.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK puts the stage and grade first on its list of questions; the NHS says treatment depends on where the cancer is, because rectal cancer often has radiotherapy and colon cancer usually does not.", source: BCUK_QUESTIONS, also: NHS_BOWEL_TREATMENT },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask when your case goes to the colorectal multidisciplinary team meeting and how you will hear what was agreed.", why: "Macmillan explains that a team of specialists meets to agree the recommended plan; NICE NG151 asks teams to give information on all treatment options available to you, with the benefits, risks and side effects.", source: MAC_MDT_BOWEL, also: NG151 },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Staging tests: a CT scan of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, an MRI of the pelvis if the cancer is in the rectum, blood tests including CEA, and sometimes a PET scan.", why: "The NHS lists the tests used to find out the size of the cancer and whether it has spread, and says it can take a few weeks to get the results.", source: NHS_BOWEL_TESTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask whether the tumour has been tested for mismatch repair or microsatellite instability, and for RAS and BRAF if the cancer has spread.", why: "NICE NG151 says to test for RAS and BRAF V600E in everyone with metastatic colorectal cancer suitable for systemic treatment, and Macmillan explains what the tests on the cancer cells look for; the mismatch repair result also starts the Lynch syndrome pathway.", source: NG151, also: MAC_CELL_TESTS },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask directly whether you are likely to need a stoma, whether it would be temporary or permanent, and arrange to meet the stoma care nurse before surgery.", why: "NICE NG151 says to advise people of the likelihood of having a stoma, why it might be necessary and for how long; Bowel Cancer UK says you meet the stoma care specialist nurse beforehand and the site may be marked in ink.", source: NG151, also: BCUK_STOMA },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "If you might want children, ask for a fertility referral before treatment starts.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK says some treatments affect fertility and the team should discuss the risk at diagnosis; pelvic radiotherapy will often cause infertility and an early menopause, so the referral cannot wait until afterwards.", source: BCUK_FERTILITY },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask about prehabilitation: exercise, diet, stopping smoking and emotional support in the weeks before treatment.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK says prehabilitation aims to improve your physical and emotional health before treatment, can reduce anxiety, improve fitness and shorten the stay in hospital, and may be offered as part of an enhanced recovery programme.", source: BCUK_PREHAB },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Before any fluorouracil or capecitabine, check that the DPD blood test has been done.", why: "Macmillan says you should have a blood test for gene changes affecting DPD before capecitabine, because low or absent DPD raises the risk of serious or life-threatening side effects and causes no symptoms of its own.", source: MAC_CAPECITABINE_CRC },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask whether a clinical trial is open to you, here or at another hospital, and what it would involve.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK says to ask your healthcare team whether you are suitable for any current trials in your area; NICE NG151 itself puts preoperative radiotherapy for early rectal cancer and deferral of surgery after a complete response inside trials and registries.", source: BCUK_TRIALS, also: NG151 },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Sort work and money: tell your employer what you choose to, ask about reasonable adjustments, and get a benefits check.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK says you are protected from discrimination from diagnosis by the Equality Act 2010, that employers must make reasonable adjustments such as part-time or flexible hours, and lists the benefits that may apply.", source: BCUK_WORK },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "If you have a stoma, practise a full bag change yourself before discharge and learn how to order supplies on prescription.", why: "Cancer Research UK says the stoma nurse teaches you to manage the stoma in hospital and that a close family member may find it useful to be there; the NHS says a permanent colostomy comes with a GP prescription for free bags, while a temporary one may have to be paid for.", source: CRUK_BOWEL_STOMA, also: NHS_COLOSTOMY_RECOVERY },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Learn the blockage and dehydration signs for your stoma, and who to ring.", why: "The NHS says a blockage is serious because the bowel could burst, and to speak to the stoma nurse urgently with cramps, sickness or swelling around the stoma, and to use 111 or an urgent GP appointment for a very high temperature, lots of blood, dehydration or severe tummy pain.", source: NHS_COLOSTOMY_COMPLICATIONS },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Start a food and symptom diary, and ask for a dietitian if eating is a problem.", why: "Cancer Research UK says to introduce one food a day after surgery and keep a diary, and lists the foods most likely to cause wind, smell or looser output; a dietitian can work out what suits you rather than guessing.", source: CRUK_BOWEL_EATING, also: BCUK_BOWEL_HABIT },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "On oxaliplatin, report tingling in the fingers, toes or throat at every visit rather than at the end of the course.", why: "NICE NG151 says to monitor prolonged sensory symptoms after platinum chemotherapy because they can be a sign the dose needs reducing to prevent permanent neuropathy; Macmillan says the symptoms are worse in the cold and to keep warm and avoid iced drinks.", source: NG151, also: MAC_OXALIPLATIN_CRC },
    { when: "Week 4 to 8", item: "Carers: tell the team you are the carer, ask the council for a free carer's assessment, and check whether Carer's Allowance applies.", why: "The NHS says a carer's assessment is free and separate from the assessment of the person you care for; Bowel Cancer UK has pages for family, friends and carers and a peer support line.", source: NHS_CARER_ASSESSMENT, also: BCUK_CARERS },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "If Lynch syndrome testing was positive or is still pending, ask what it means for your treatment, for surveillance, and for testing your relatives.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK says testing should be offered at diagnosis because the condition can affect treatment options, that each child, brother and sister then has a 50 percent chance of carrying it, and that surveillance means colonoscopy every 18 months to two years.", source: BCUK_LYNCH },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your follow-up plan after curative surgery: CEA blood tests and CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis for the first 3 years.", why: "NICE NG151 says to offer follow-up for detection of local recurrence and distant metastases for the first 3 years, including serum carcinoembryonic antigen and CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis.", source: NG151 },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Have the printed red cards, the medicine list, the stoma or bowel routine and the 24-hour number to hand, and know who to talk to when it gets hard.", why: "Bowel Cancer UK runs an Ask the Nurse service, a peer support line and online communities; NICE NG151 asks teams to give advice on how, when and where to seek help if side effects become problematic.", source: BCUK_CARERS, also: NG151 },
  ],
  "lung-cancer": LUNG_CHECKLIST,
  nsclc: LUNG_CHECKLIST,
  sclc: [
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask when you will see the oncologist: with small-cell lung cancer the standard is within a week of the decision to recommend treatment.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.8.1) says to arrange for people with small-cell lung cancer to have an assessment by a thoracic oncologist within 1 week of deciding to recommend treatment.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Get the name and number of your lung cancer clinical nurse specialist and the hospital's 24-hour line.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.1.1) says a lung cancer clinical nurse specialist should be available at all stages of care to support people and, as appropriate, their family members or carers, and (1.19.3) that people should know how to contact them between hospital visits.", source: NG122_CNS },
    { when: "Day 1 to 7", item: "Ask whether this is limited stage or extensive stage, and what each means for the plan.", why: "NICE NG122 writes separate recommendations for the two: limited stage (1.10) means 4 to 6 cycles of cisplatin-based chemotherapy with radiotherapy to the chest given during the first or second cycle, and extensive stage (1.11) means platinum-based chemotherapy with immunotherapy, up to 6 cycles depending on response and toxicity.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask whether radiotherapy to the chest will be given at the same time as chemotherapy, and whether it is once or twice a day.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.10.2) says to offer twice-daily radiotherapy with concurrent chemotherapy to people with limited-stage disease and a performance status of 0 or 1 whose disease fits in a radical thoracic radiotherapy volume, starting during the first or second cycle, and (1.10.3) to offer once-daily radiotherapy if that is declined or not possible.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Ask about preventive radiotherapy to the brain, including the downsides, and about the alternative of regular MRI scans.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.10.5) says to offer prophylactic cranial irradiation at 25 Gy in 10 fractions for limited-stage disease with a performance status of 0 to 2 that has not progressed, and (1.11.5) to consider it in extensive-stage disease that responded; NICE also says it can adversely affect quality of life, that the survival benefits are limited, and has an open research question comparing it with routine MRI follow-up.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
    { when: "Week 1 to 2", item: "Put the red cards on the fridge now rather than later, because things change quickly with this cancer.", why: "The NHS says to call 999 if you are struggling to breathe or coughing up more than a few spots or streaks of blood; Macmillan says to ring the 24-hour number for a temperature above 37.5 C or below 36 C or for feeling unwell with a normal temperature, and that swelling of the face, neck or arms needs contacting the team straight away.", source: NHS_LUNG_SYMPTOMS, also: MAC_LUNG_PEMBRO },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Ask for a palliative care referral alongside treatment, for symptom control rather than instead of treatment.", why: "Marie Curie says palliative care can be offered at any point after a diagnosis and alongside treatments aimed at controlling the illness such as chemotherapy or radiotherapy; the randomised evidence for starting early in lung cancer comes from metastatic non-small-cell disease, where it improved quality of life, mood and survival.", source: MARIE_CURIE_PALLIATIVE, also: TEMEL_2010_LUNG },
    { when: "Week 2 to 4", item: "Do the practical paperwork while you feel well: work, sick pay, benefits, and anything you would want sorted.", why: "Roy Castle Lung Cancer Foundation's living with lung cancer material has a getting organised section on sorting paperwork and putting affairs in order, framed as regaining control after a diagnosis; Cancer Research UK says getting help with practical things early stops them becoming a bigger issue later.", source: ROY_CASTLE_LIVING, also: CRUK_LUNG_COPING },
    { when: "Week 3 to 6", item: "Ask what the scan after treatment will show and what happens next, including what is offered if the cancer comes back.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.12.1) says to offer people with relapsed small-cell lung cancer assessment by a thoracic oncologist, (1.12.2) to inform people whose disease did not respond to first-line treatment that there is very limited evidence that second-line chemotherapy will benefit them, and (1.12.5) to offer radiotherapy for palliation of local symptoms.", source: NG122_MANAGEMENT },
    { when: "By day 60", item: "Know your follow-up and who runs it, and find the support that fits you.", why: "NICE NG122 (1.19.1) says to offer an initial follow-up appointment with a specialist within 6 weeks of completing treatment and regular appointments after that; Roy Castle offers a telephone line staffed by lung cancer nurses, information days, support groups and an online community for everyone affected by lung cancer.", source: NG122_FOLLOW_UP, also: ROY_CASTLE_SUPPORT },
  ],
};
